# Design Document Guidelines

## Writing Standards

- Design documents should be concise, factual, sober, and concrete
- Try to balance half prose + half code snippets or math
- Include links to relevant files, and brief quotations where appropriate
- Do not guess time estimates. Do not guess numbers, e.g. speedups. Limit to known facts

## Required Document Format

All design documents must follow this exact structure:

### Document Template

```markdown
# Title
## Objective
Single concise sentence describing the goal (the why, not the how).

## Background
### One background subsection per relevant topic, typically 2-5 background subsections total
Each background section should be one or a few paragraphs with links and code snippets or math.

## Design Overview
A single paragraph + code snippet or math describing the high level approach of the design

## Design Details
### One details subsection per relevant nuance or technical issue, typically 3-6 subsections total

## Work Plan
A concise - [ ] todo list of 5-10 todo items. Each todo item is a single concise sentence describing one task, the size of about one git commit.
```

## Section Guidelines

- **Title**: Clear, specific name for the design
- **Objective**: Single sentence stating the goal (why, not how)
- **Background**: 2-5 subsections with paragraphs, links, and code/math
- **Design Overview**: One paragraph + code/math for high-level approach  
- **Design Details**: 3-6 subsections covering technical nuances
- **Work Plan**: 5-10 concise todo items, each about one commit size

## Content Requirements

- Include links to relevant source files
- Add brief quotations from code where appropriate
- Balance prose with code snippets and mathematical expressions
- Stick to known facts - avoid speculation on timelines or performance gains
- Keep all content factual and concrete

